<p>A few days ago I pushed a new library for making charts interactively in Emacs to <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot" data-cached-time="2024-07-01T00:02:33" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/ewpDPTtg1.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a>.  One suggestion was to use <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/eplot/issues/3" data-cached-time="2024-07-01T00:02:37" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/ewpZdLZj5.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">transient or something</a> to allow customising the look of the charts instead of adding headers to the data buffer.</p>
<p>Which makes a lot of sense &#8212; you don&#8217;t want to alter the data just to plot something.  But on the other hand, I didn&#8217;t really know what it&#8217;d look like &#8212; for UX stuff, you really have to just try.</p>
<p>Behold!  The most thrilling video in the history of videos!  That are thrilling!</p>
